      "The halved /apply conversion is substantially an artefact of who is being counted, not a form that stopped working \u2014 and one traffic source's decline is entirely composition.",
    detail:
      "MP-538 proved the /apply decline is real and left \u201cwhy\u201d open; MP-540 removed page speed. Three more leads were removed by measurement here rather than built on. It is NOT device-specific (desktop 17.3%% -> 8.7%%, mobile 23.1%% -> 14.9%% \u2014 both fell by a similar factor, and recent desktop is 23 sessions with 2 conversions, which cannot carry a claim either way), which refutes the desktop-vs-mobile lead MP-538 handed forward. It is NOT a deploy step-change: the weekly series slides gradually from 36.6%% in early June to ~13%% in September, and a code regression produces a step. What it IS: concentrated by traffic source, and badly confounded by the denominator. A converted session must emit a second page_view because /apply/success is its own URL, so a session with exactly one page_view cannot convert by construction \u2014 0 of 878 such sessions converted in 4.5 months. That population grew, and unevenly: for Instagram/Meta traffic it went from 42.1%% to 68.6%% of sessions. Holding it constant changes the story. Among sessions that produced more than one page_view, the 'other' source is FLAT (35.5%% -> 33.3%%) while its blended rate fell 23.0%% -> 14.3%%, so that entire decline is composition and nothing about the form changed for those people. Meta/social is down for real but half as far as it looked (37.7%% -> 18.5%%, not 21.8%% -> 5.8%%). v_apply_funnel_by_source publishes the split and REPORTS single_pageview_sessions rather than subtracting them: a real human who lands and leaves is a real conversion failure and belongs in the denominator, a prefetch is not, and one page_view cannot tell them apart \u2014 separating them needs MP-539's apply_field_first_input, which is days old. The view ships with no verdict column on purpose, because recent per-source counts are 3-86 sessions and any threshold over them flips on a single applicant. Also fixed a real defect found on the way: /apply's \u201cdebounced\u201d sessionStorage write never debounced. react-hook-form discards whatever a watch callback returns, so the clearTimeout cleanup was dead code \u2014 proven 3 callback fires / 0 cleanup calls against the real library \u2014 and every keystroke scheduled a timer that nobody cancelled. The timer now lives outside the callback, and the submit guard is re-checked when it fires rather than only when it is scheduled, which also closes a narrow window where a write already in flight could restore an applicant's details into storage that submit had just cleared. Six tests import the real module, one of them pinning react-hook-form's actual behaviour so the original pattern cannot come back silently; three mutation proofs, each asserted to have landed and to be non-inert before its verdict was read. MP-541.",
