    detail:
      "Demo mode exists so Sam can show the platform to recruits, prospective agents and partners without disclosing the book. Turning it on (?demo=1, remembered afterwards) mounts a banner reading 'Demo mode \u2014 every number and name on screen is fake. Nothing here is live client data.' PROVEN FALSE against live production in a real browser: the money rendered under that banner is byte-identical to the money rendered without it (\u0024 20,695 / 19,118 / 19,062 / 12,425), and the page still showed real producers OBIAJULU, EDWIN, WENDELL and CHUDI, a real hire's manager, and a real applicant's city. The mask itself was never broken \u2014 it is well built and has 15 tests. The bug sat one line upstream, in the gate deciding what reached it: it rewrote a response only when the method was GET, and supabase-js sends every .rpc() as POST. All four landing RPCs are POST, so 178 .rpc() call sites across the platform came back unmasked while 1,467 .from() selects were masked. The gate's comment defended itself with writes \u2014 'a POST during a demo still writes real data' \u2014 which is a correct reason never to alter a request and never to block a write, and not a reason to skip masking what a READ carried over POST puts on screen. A read carried over POST is still a read. It survived because maskPayload was thoroughly tested and the gate in front of it had no test at all; a correct mask behind a wrong gate is indistinguishable from no mask. A second defect was only visible because the fix was measured end to end rather than at the seam: the landing ticker returns the producer's real first name under a bare 'agent' key, which the name list never matched, so repairing the gate alone would have left the banner's 'and name' clause still false on the exact surface that proved the bug. Now: GET anywhere, plus POST to an RPC or an edge function, are masked; a POST straight at a table stays a real write and renders exactly what was stored. 12 new tests on the gate and 3 mutation proofs \u2014 restoring the GET-only rule reddens the RPC tests, masking every method reddens the write-protection tests, and removing the agent key reddens the ticker test. Refused the loud version: the landing RPCs are public and anon-readable, so nothing is newly exposed to the internet and no money is owed \u2014 the cost is Sam acting on a false assurance in front of someone he chose to show the platform to. MP-530.",
