      "When the backend is unreachable, the login page shows an amber notice so a person who cannot get in is not left staring at a bare 'invalid login' toast. That notice ended with: 'This page will sign you in automatically the moment it recovers.' Measured, not assumed: Login.tsx has zero useEffect hooks and zero onAuthStateChange subscriptions, and the banner exports nothing but its route test, so there is no channel by which a recovery could reach the sign-in form. The sentence was false the day it shipped, and it was addressed to the one person who cannot tell it is false. Worse, recovery rendered as the notice silently vanishing, so the only signal they had disappeared with no instruction. Making the promise true would mean holding their password in memory across an outage and replaying it, which is a worse thing than the bug, so the promise was narrowed to one the page can actually keep: it says 'Leave this page open. It will tell you the moment the servers are back', and on recovery it replaces the amber bar with a green 'Servers are back. Try signing in again.' The recovery notice only appears for an outage that was actually shown and not dismissed, and a fresh outage clears it. Signed-in sessions are deliberately unchanged, since their next query simply works. 24 tests, 5 new, and three mutation proofs that kill disjoint sets: reverting the recovery notice leaves the false-promise test green and reverting the copy leaves the recovery tests green, so neither half can speak for the other. MP-522.",
