    detail:
      "Claimed the oldest open item, carried unworked by three waves: were all historical health-probe failures the same cause as the 2026-09-11 Supabase outage? REFUTED the inherited hypothesis by measuring it. SupabaseHealthBanner's probe armed its 6000ms AbortController and sampled performance.now() ABOVE `await import(supabase/client)`, so the 44,816-byte gz vendor-supabase chunk it pulls on its first run was counted as database latency -- and the landing page genuinely does not preload that chunk, so the probe IS the first importer there. But at Lighthouse's ACTUAL conditions the cost is 62-131ms against a 3000ms bar, because --throttling-method=simulate leaves the network fast and only slows the CPU. So it cannot explain the historical CI failure. What can: probe.jsonl, an instrument that already existed, records FIVE data-path failures on 2026-09-04 (landing RPCs HTTP 500 at 14:25, 16:59, 18:03, a CONFIRMED CRIT at 19:57, three more at 20:20) and commit 0925fc38 landed at 20:02:07Z -- five minutes after a confirmed CRIT. Both known historical failures sit inside real faults. Kept the caveat rather than smoothing it: the banner probes the system_settings TABLE while those CRITs are RPC 500s, so this is 'consistent with', not 'identical to'. FIXED the real defect found on the way: at 2G the probe computed 5361ms of which 5278ms was its own download while the query took 85ms, and at edge speeds the import alone blew the 6000ms abort and rendered 'The database is not answering' about a database answering in 85ms. MP-430 saw this symptom on Sam's building Wi-Fi and debounced it to two failures; that helps 'down' (the second probe pays no download) but 'slow' arms on the FIRST probe with no debounce and renders the same banner. The timer was measuring the wrong interval. A failed import now leaves state and failStreak untouched -- we could not look, so we say nothing, rather than spending a strike on our own fault. LATENT and says so: on every route where the probe is armed today the chunk is already loaded (measured on /login at 2G, no false banner in 30s) and the landing page has been disarmed since MP-515. Caught my own fix introducing an unhandled rejection on every probe (Promise.race's loser rejecting at 20s with nobody listening). 19/19 tests with 2 new cases that go RED on the old ordering, 3 mutation proofs each asserted to have landed, and a new check:probe-timer-span wired into verify:core AND pre-commit. MP-521.",
