      "The amber \"Backend is sluggish\" bar was mounting itself over the public recruiting page ~36 seconds into every visit and shoving the entire page down 61 pixels. Layout shift on the landing page was 0.0741 against a 0.05 budget \u2014 48% over. The CI gate had reported it twice and been talked out of it both times.",
    detail:
      "PROVEN, not inferred, and the inherited explanation was refuted first. The previous wave dismissed this failure as a CI artifact: 'the gate is named Post-deploy but triggers on PUSH, so it grades the PREVIOUS commit BY CONSTRUCTION.' That is false. Measured across the 20 most recent deploys \u2014 push time from the workflow run, ready time from the GitHub deployment record Vercel writes at its ready instant, cross-checked against the Vercel API on the failing deploy itself (created 15:14:07.987Z, ready 15:14:29.638Z) \u2014 the deploy lag is min 17s / p50 20s / max 21s against a 90s wait. 0 of 20 runs could have measured the previous deploy, and the worst case still had 69s of headroom. The operand behind the false refutation was a LOCAL build's entry hash, which is not a valid predictor of what Vercel builds and is not a commit identifier at all, since a change confined to a lazily chunked module leaves the entry hash untouched \u2014 the identical error the wave before it had already recorded in its own commit message. So the budget failure was correctly attributed all along, and the shift stayed on Sam's public page. ROOT CAUSE, reproduced exactly: SupabaseHealthBanner arms a 30-second timer, the probe fails, and the banner mounts in normal document flow above everything, displacing the page container from [0,0,412,823] to [0,61,412,762]. One shift, 0.0741, identical rects on a GitHub runner and on a laptop across four runs, and the banner's DOM index matches the node path Lighthouse named. The 30s delay was deliberate \u2014 its comment reads 'so Lighthouse audits (typically 6-10s of simulated load) never trigger' it \u2014 but it was calibrated against the WRONG CLOCK: 6-10s is Lighthouse's simulated metric time, while the timer is real wall clock and a mobile audit of this site runs 46.9s of it. The mitigation never applied. FIXED by arming the probe only for a signed-in session, which is the only session that can act on the answer: a signed-out visitor on the recruiting page cannot do anything about 'Postgres data plane unresponsive' except read an outage bar over Sam's landing page and watch it jump. A longer timer was rejected because it only moves the failure to a slower audit. MUTATION-PROVEN BOTH DIRECTIONS on two real builds served locally: with the fix reverted, CLS 0.0741 and the 61px banner present; with the fix, CLS 0.0000 and the banner absent. REFUSED a much louder finding mid-wave \u2014 a direct curl of the probe's own query returned HTTP 522 three times, which reads as 'the database is down', and the control killed it: every endpoint 522s from this machine including ones the browser loads fine, so it was this sandbox's egress being challenged, not an outage. The gate also now proves what it measured before it measures it, polling /version.json (which the app has shipped since August and nothing ever read) until production serves this exact commit, and naming that build in the failure line. If the match never arrives it declines to run rather than going red, because failing there would be a verdict about a page nobody measured. Four branches proven against stubs, including a garbage response and a response carrying the right id under the wrong key \u2014 both correctly refuse to manufacture a match. MP-515.",
    commit: "mp-515-landing-cls-health-banner",
