      "MP-388 found the AgentLink sync's 1,068-row rewrites stampeding the dashboard and shipped a coalescer for them. The drops kept coming, and MP-427 then measured them ANTI-correlated with sync hours: the three heaviest sync hours dropped zero scheduled runs, while the four worst stall hours moved zero deals. So the remaining storm was not the sync. This is what it was. invalidateOperationalTruth() ends by dispatching the window event \"production-realtime-update\" — correct for a deal post or a new hire, which need to refresh every other surface in the tab immediately. useProductionRealtime() LISTENS for that same event. AgentCloudHome wired the listener straight back into the dispatcher, so handling the event re-emitted it: a ring that re-drove itself every 350ms debounce forever, entirely inside the browser, never passing through the row-change path the coalescer guards. That is why no amount of row coalescing could see it. Measured on the real modules under fake timers: one seed event produced 29 laps in ten seconds of wall clock with zero row changes — 174 laps a minute, each one invalidating apex_admin_home_dashboard, scoped_production_scoreboard and imo_by_agency_period. The actual database rate settles at the RPC latency, which is the ~45/min the exposure probe recorded. It shipped 2026-08-27 in 55b494e4 and ran until today. The fix is an explicit broadcast flag: mutation callers still echo (four of them depend on it, and a test holds that), the listener does not. Because a unit test can only grade wiring it declares itself, a source guard now reads the real call sites — 19 of them — and fails the build on a listener that re-emits, with no baseline count, because a count-only floor lets a regression be laundered by an unrelated pay-down. Proven four ways: restoring the pre-fix line verbatim goes red and names the file and line, the same call inside a comment stays green, and a scanner that can no longer find what it grades refuses to vouch for the tree instead of passing.",
