      "apex-doctor had carried this CRITICAL for three waves — MP-370 falsified the worker pool, MP-371 falsified slot exhaustion, MP-374 falsified fire load — and the report honestly said the cause was not established. Measured: deals carries 229,200 updates on 1,817 live rows (the sync upsert rewrites every row whether or not it changed, without touching updated_at, which is why updated_at-based counts read 0). Each rewrite is a postgres_changes event on the shared production-global-shared realtime channel; the per-subscriber 800ms throttle only bounded one subscriber, so ~1,068 events became ~150 refetches per sync of apex_admin_home_dashboard (2.2-4.5s), scoped_production_scoreboard and imo_by_agency_period. The exposure probe recorded 139 home calls in a single 10-minute window and 187 statement timeouts in 24h; the drop instants sit inside the sync windows, and pg_cron's launcher blew its 10-second start deadline inside synchronous writes to a never-pruned 528 MB cron.job_run_details. Three fixes. (1) Row changes are coalesced at the channel before they become a window event: 2s quiet period, 30s ceiling, so a single posted deal still refreshes within 2s and a bulk sync refreshes at most ~5 times instead of ~150. Proven with fake timers on a 1,068-row burst and mutation-proven (removing the coalescer fails 3 tests). (2) cron.job_run_details gets a nightly 14-day prune, bounded to 200k rows a run — proven live on a 13,605-row batch at ~620 rows/s; the ~2.6M backlog converges over roughly two weeks of nights, which is stated rather than hidden. (3) Check #44, the exposure verdict, was reporting UNPROVEN 'nobody exercised the admin dashboard' on the very rows that showed the stampede, because two legacy keys MP-361 removed could never clear its call floor and any thin key vetoed the verdict; it now grades the heavy keys' call RATE (8/min band against a measured 12-14/min disease), so a return of the storm reads REGRESSED. One window (16:20-16:31Z, 123 calls, no sync) is not explained by this cause and is recorded as such.",
