      "Three things were wrong under a rail that only looked right. (1) The board filtered created_at to the current Phoenix month, so a hire stuck at pre-licensed since July was invisible on the one screen built for unsticking people, and on the 1st of each month the whole board resets to near-zero. (2) It put producers in the licensing lane: the rail matched the stage string against /(training|onboard|contract)/ and /(field|active|production|ready)/, and 'evaluated' matches neither, so it fell through to step 1, \"Licensed\". Measured, 8 of 8 active agents sitting at 'evaluated' have a first deal AND rows in the book — every one of them a proven earner rendered as somebody still waiting on a license. 'evaluated' is now the top rung, placed on that evidence rather than on the word. (3) The \"next action\" line printed the raw onboarding_stage column with underscores swapped for spaces, truncated to \"Onb…\" in the space it was given; it restated where the person was and never said what to do. It now names the first unmet gate — get them licensed, book the onboarding call, move them into the course, get the first deal — computed from license status, ladder rank, whether a call is actually on the calendar, and whether a first deal exists. New: public.fn_hire_stage_rank + src/lib/hireLadder.ts write the ladder explicitly because the enum's own sort order is not one ('live' sorts before 'onboarding', 'evaluated' sorts last), and a test asserts the two definitions carry the same pairs rather than re-implementing the mapping and agreeing with itself — mutation-proven, 3 of 7 tests go red when the TS side is changed. public.v_hire_launch_board carries every signal one card needs in one read: rank, days parked in stage, next onboarding call, course modules done, book production, stalled-past-14-days, and the computed next action. advance_hire_stage and set_hire_license_status are the two writes, both optimistic on the value the browser last saw so two managers working the same board cannot silently overwrite each other, both restricted to admin or the hire's own inviting manager (the same set the existing row-level policy already admits, deliberately not the CRM's read scope, which lets VA staff read the whole roster), and both write an agent_stage_moves receipt — until now agents carried only stage_changed_at, so who moved a hire was unrecoverable. Every move that can send mail says so in the confirmation before it is made, naming the address: marking somebody licensed queues their course and Discord emails, and the dialog reports which ones are actually pending rather than predicting the trigger. The card also carries what the person actually DOES, composed from the v_hire_activity view a parallel wave landed the same morning rather than duplicated: Jaden Selvaraj shows 1,196 dialer calls and 100 conversations in 30 days against no recorded stage at all — the old card rendered him as \"Onboarding\" with nothing to click. The board also now calls out hires writing business while still marked unlicensed — Marquay Vaughns has 35 deals in the book against an unlicensed status and no stage at all. CAUGHT BEFORE SHIPPING: the first cut of the view leaked. A non-security-invoker view runs as its owner, and the agents policy admits every logged-in account for the leaderboard, so a plain producing agent read all 34 hires and 29 of their email addresses. Re-measured after gating it on the CRM scope contract: admin 34, plain agent 1 — himself.",
