      "ntfy.sh is Sam's primary phone push, and it was silently dead for every alert whose subject carried an emoji — which is every licensed-agent celebration (\"\u{1F393} <name> passed their license exam\") and every \u{1F534} critical. HTTP header values are ByteStrings: Deno throws while constructing the Request on any codepoint above 0xFF, so the push never reached the network. The alert was then marked delivered anyway, because Discord and email had landed. 389 alerts carry a non-ASCII subject and 155 of them are recorded as sent, including 71 of 71 non-ASCII criticals.",
    detail:
      "The premise inverted for the twelfth consecutive wave, and re-measuring is what caught it. The obvious reading of the symptom — postNtfy looks up its topic with .maybeSingle(), the exact call that broke the SMS carrier lookup four hours earlier in ed01a7de — was FALSIFIED: system_settings holds zero ntfy_topic_url rows, so maybeSingle() returns cleanly, the hardcoded fallback is used, and that fallback matches the topic six working local scripts already post to. The URL was never wrong. The real cause was one line further down, in the Title header. Proven both directions before writing the fix: in Deno 2.9.5 a raw emoji title throws TypeError 'not a valid ByteString' while an ASCII one returns 200, and the throw happens inside postNtfy's own try, whose `catch { return false }` reported it as an ordinary false with no reason attached — the same swallow that hid the SMS bug. Fixed with RFC 2047 encoded-words rather than by stripping the emoji, because ntfy decodes them: verified live that the encoded title round-trips back to '\u{1F393} ...' on the wire, so nothing is lost. postNtfy now returns a receipt ('ok' | 'http:<status>' | 'error:<message>') instead of a bare boolean, and a failure is recorded in the error string instead of vanishing. A second divergence was fixed in the same function: the ad-hoc path marked an alert sent only when email or SMS landed, while flush() used any-channel-landed, so a Discord/ntfy-only delivery stayed sent_at NULL forever and got re-dispatched on the next flush. Note the honest limit of the blast radius — 'marked sent' means another channel did land, so Sam may well have seen these in Discord; the precise claim is that the one channel that reaches his pocket never fired, and nothing recorded that. Guarded twice: a deno test that imports the real encoder from _shared/header-safe.ts (a copy-pasted assertion would pass while production broke) and which also asserts the NAIVE construction still throws, so if a future Deno stops throwing the test fails loudly instead of quietly protecting nothing; and apex-doctor Check #21, which drives the real deployed encoder over a throwaway ntfy topic so the weekly run never pushes to Sam's phone. All five of the check's branches were exercised, including a regression branch matching Deno's error string verbatim, and 'unknown' is reported as not-clean rather than as healthy.",
