    label:
      "Every deal closed at APEX was publishing the client's real full name into a Discord channel. notify-deal-submitted built a \"\u{1F464} Client\" embed field from client_first_name + client_last_name and posted it on every non-draft deal insert \u2014 243 deals in the preceding 30 days, all 243 carrying a name, the newest two hours before the fix. Removed and deployed live (function version 43, verified by reading the deployed source back, not by trusting the deploy output). A win post is agent + carrier + product + money. Never who bought it.",
    detail:
      "The documented Discord scope for this platform has always been deals/apps/hires/milestones and never PII, so this was a live violation of a rule that was already written down. Closed the same way the rest of this codebase closes things \u2014 with a ratchet \u2014 but the ratchet is the interesting part. A grep for client_first_name inside the embed literal would not have caught this: the PII was laundered through a local (const clientName) seventeen lines before the embed, and the embed itself was assigned to a variable and only passed to the webhook later. The first version of the guard did exactly that naive check, reported \"0 leaks\" against a tree with the bug deliberately reintroduced, and would have shipped as a guard that fails the single case it exists for \u2014 a fake-success gate protecting against fake success. Rewritten as a fixed-point taint analysis: any local derived from a client-identity field is tainted, anything built from a tainted local is tainted transitively, and a violation fires when a tainted name reaches an outbound webhook payload. It now reports the full chain (\"embed reaches an outbound webhook payload and is derived from client_first_name via clientName\") and was proven against the exact code that shipped. Scope covers 8 webhook-posting edge functions and 12 client-identity fields; agent and profile fields are deliberately excluded because naming the agent is the entire point of a win post. CORRECTION (appended 2026-08-11, after this entry was already live): this wave also claimed it had \"swept for the same class elsewhere and found none\", specifically that no database function paired Discord with client names. That was FALSE, and the very next commit proved it — a71e321c found trg_fn_deal_celebration building its Discord embed TITLE from NEW.client_first_name || NEW.client_last_name, firing on every first-deal-of-the-day and every deal at or above $3,000 AOP, with 464 of 1,759 deals matching the gate. So the leak was closed in the edge function while a second live path kept publishing names for several more hours. The sweep was not wrong by accident: it filtered on functions whose body contains the literal string 'discord' AND client PII, and a function that fetches its webhook URL from a settings key never has to spell the vendor's name. Requiring a keyword the code need not contain turns a sweep into a coin flip and reports the result as certainty. The correct test is PII plus ANY outbound sink, with no keyword filter — which is what apex-doctor Check #16 does (net.http_post OR discord, not AND), and re-running it against pg_proc now returns clean: 11 public functions reference client identity, none of them has an HTTP sink of any kind. The narrower claims in this wave did hold: the plaque embed carries only agent + premium, notify-deal-alert emails a single internal address, and the deal_sync_log audit payload records agent_name/monthly/aop/product/source only. The lesson is the one this codebase keeps relearning — a clean result from a search that could not have found the thing is not evidence of absence.",
