      "The interesting half of this was on mobile. Compacting the component alone moved the phone from 253px to 239px — a 6% change that looked like the work had failed. The cause was not the component: index.css's `@media (max-width: 640px)` block forced `.apex-page-header h1 { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 9vw, 2.15rem) !important }`, which is 34.4px at a 390px viewport, and because it was !important it beat the component's own text-xl outright. The same block also re-applied `padding-block: 1.1rem` over the new py-3.5. So the compaction was genuinely live and correct on desktop while the device Sam actually runs the business on saw almost nothing — and a guard that only read the .tsx would have reported total success. The mobile h1 rule is now scoped to the header (5.5vw, capped at 1.5rem) and wins on specificity, while landing/marketing h1s keep the large clamp they were tuned for. Other changes: the eyebrow chip moved inline with the title instead of stacking its own row (where most of the desktop height went), its type came up from 11px to 14px since it was the smallest text in the app and is metadata an operator is meant to read, and it is hidden below sm where it wrapped to a 29px row of its own; the subtitle clamps to one line on phones; the title is 24px desktop / 21.45px mobile. The `apex-header-scan` rail — a 3.8s infinite alternate animation running on every route purely for decoration — is gone; the global prefers-reduced-motion block at index.css:224 made it accessible, which is not the same as making it earn the space. Honest limit recorded in the component: desktop meets the 72-96px target, mobile does not meet 64-80px and structurally cannot, because a 44px minimum touch target for the actions slot plus 28px of padding is already a 72px floor before a single character of title exists. New ratchet check:page-header-compact guards all of it — padding step, title size, the scan rail, and specifically the merged-selector regression that made this desktop-only — and it was itself caught reporting two false positives on first run (its own docstring, and the legitimate letter-spacing rule at index.css:1915) and fixed to strip comments and require font-size before firing.",
