      "The dangerous part of this one was the proposed FIX, not the bug. The rebuild brief diagnosed it as 'an aggregate Combined row is being treated like an individual carrier' and prescribed filtering that row out. Combined is not an aggregate — it is {id:3} in agentlink_carriers, an ordinary row beside American Amicable, Foresters and Transamerica (Combined Insurance is a real company), and it holds $130,625.88 of 30-day premium: 46.6% of the book and the agency's single largest carrier concentration. Shipping the prescribed fix would have deleted the top carrier from analytics while leaving the actual bug live — the remaining ten rows still sum to 253%. The decisive tell was already on the page: v_business_analytics_insights renders Combined at a correct 46.6% because that view divides by team_premium_30d, so the same carrier printed two different shares (46.6% and 220.8%) that differ only in the denominator's window. Every operand was re-measured live against xrzweoneiieddzxogewk rather than inherited from the brief — the third recorded instance in this repo of an audit's hypothesis being wrong in a way that would have shipped (see the 2026-08-07 week-over-week source mismatch). Guarded by a new window-parity rule in check-metric-truth.mjs: the existing parity ratchets all police the SOURCE of two operands (legacy deals vs agentlink_book), so a single-source/two-window ratio passed all of them. The new rule asserts the denominator is row-derived and fails the build if the share expression reaches for the MTD figure again; proven both directions before commit (passes on the fix, fires on a reintroduced regression). Also in this wave: migration 20260811030000_apex_career_toolkit_workflows applied live and recorded in supabase_migrations.schema_migrations — its four tables (apex_toolkit_agents, apex_agent_journeys, apex_agent_journey_steps, apex_toolkit_agent_contact_log) did not exist in the database while the uncommitted LicensedInbox.tsx already read AND wrote apex_toolkit_agents and threw on error, so shipping the page first would have been a hard Licensed Inbox outage. The pre-commit enum guard then blocked that same commit with what looked like a third bug — applications.status = 'active' — and it was wrong: that filter belongs to the next chain in the same Promise.all, against apex_toolkit_agents, whose status column is CHECK (status IN ('active','hired','passed')). Editing the page to satisfy the guard would have broken a correct query. The guard had two attribution bugs instead: its receiver was hardcoded to `supabase`, so chains built on a typed wrapper (toolkitInboxClient) were never scanned at all — a silent gap rather than a false pass — and neither of its regexes tolerated a generic parameter, so .from<Omit<LicensedRow, 'origin'>>('apex_toolkit_agents') did not register as a chain boundary and the preceding applications chain swallowed its filters. Both fixed; coverage went from 348 to 353 chains, and the guard was re-proven in both directions (fires on a seeded 'terminated' literal, silent on correct code).",
