      "wave-cron-tick-honest-green: the workflow that keeps the sync pipelines moving when pg_cron dies had been painting itself green no matter what happened. Yesterday it was failing every tick and someone stopped the red by marking the failing job non-critical — but every job in that step was already non-critical and wrapped in `|| true`, so after that change nothing could fail it at all. A tick this morning had InsuraCloud return a 502 with ok:false and the run still reported success. The part that actually mattered was not the known-dead endpoint: when the automation token died two days ago, all six sync calls would have failed and every run would still have shown green while pg_cron was down and no pipeline moved. A green tick now means the pipelines actually moved — any failure outside one named, known-dead upstream turns the run red and pushes to Sam's phone. The dead endpoint itself was being hit 288 times a day and writing about 200 error rows a day into the same table whose 465 fake-success rows are the reason this codebase distrusts the colour green; it is now an hourly probe that will announce the moment the endpoints come back.",
